# Expanded Cinema, retrospective

Closed by Joel on 2026-09-15 after roughly 48 hours of maker time on a local
model, with an art director session steering from 2026-09-14 onward.

## What was made

| measure | value |
| --- | --- |
| commits | 290 |
| ledger rows | 180 |
| study files | 10 |
| studies that passed the hard floor on the director's own frames | 2 (withhold-01, colour-01); clock-04 v18 passed four of five lines |
| ledger rows spent on clock-04 alone | 57 |
| false "verified" cuts caught by the director | 6 |
| local tokens through the maker | about 600M |

The gallery stays live on colour-01: the 1943 film split into red, green, and
blue with 3 and 6 second delays on a curved screen in a coloured room, the
past held on the wall.

## What worked

- **The MCP pointer path.** Content reached the site at runtime with one
  writable key; code pushed only when the site changed. This never broke.
- **Archive URLs per sha.** Every candidate had a stable address, so
  verification could happen before the cut. Once the maker used it, cuts
  stopped being the way to check work.
- **Eyes-first direction.** Ten sweeps judged from the ledger called a static
  title card "ok". The moment the director took two screenshots per sweep and
  wrote a five-line hard floor, the work changed within an hour.
- **Branch from the shipped file.** colour-01 lost ninety minutes to a fresh
  scene whose plate was never in frame; it rendered in one commit once it was
  a copy of withhold-01 with one change.
- **Probe builds.** Solid magenta materials settled placement in one load
  every time, where parameter twiddling never did.
- **Root-cause finds by the maker.** Range serving for video seeks, the
  seek-guard that re-seeked every frame, the reveal and arm loops, the cloned
  CanvasTexture that never updated. Each was real and each was found by
  logging state per second, not by reasoning.
- **Continuous cadence and ship-then-start.** The daily cap was the wrong
  grain; twenty versions of clock-04 in a day taught more than the previous
  two days.

## What failed

- **The builder judged itself.** Six cuts went live "verified" on frames that
  were blank tabs or that plainly failed the gates. A small model reading its
  own screenshot against its own claim says yes. The director's attention
  became the only real QA, at thirty-minute cadence.
- **Every study re-solved playback.** Seek, reveal, arm, texture update, Range
  support: each study rebuilt the apparatus and broke it in a new place.
  Roughly half the ledger is infrastructure, not images.
- **One clip.** All studies used the same ninety seconds of Conquer by the
  Clock. The material never pushed the work; the ingest pipeline failed on
  archive.org URLs early and was not revisited.
- **The grain was too big.** A "study" was a scene with a rail, a beam, a
  wall, a reveal, and a shader, and every version touched several of them. No
  version could be compared to the last on one variable.
- **The default look was a debug scene.** Grey room, white additive beam,
  flat basic materials, no lights, no shaders. Colour and node materials
  arrived only after Joel asked for radical changes on day three, and then
  took six hours to land.
- **Verification tooling was shared and flaky.** The director and the maker
  collided on one agent-browser daemon for four sweeps; the maker cut twice on
  the resulting blank tabs.
- **Direction reached the maker through three channels** (intercom, the
  Brain, Joel directly) and the director did not see one of them, which cost
  a wrong correction.
- **Idle after ship.** The maker ended its turn after every cut, chain, and
  honest no-cut until reminded, five times.

## What to keep, verbatim

- The hard floor (space, visible apparatus, motion in five seconds, film as
  material, filled frame) and the three critique gates (frames differ, no
  title card, point to the operation in the 16s frame).
- The Material rule: palette with a source, projector light colours the
  room, surfaces with relief, one TSL node material per study.
- The stance page's provocations with sources, especially the ones not yet
  tried: the beam is the film, the surface is the film, the Ganzfeld room,
  the image lands on a thing, the phone is the projector.

# Successor proposal: a tournament, not a diary

The change is structural. Separate the roles, lock the apparatus, shrink the
grain, and make the machine the judge.

## Shape

1. **A locked rig.** One proven scene harness as a library: room, projector,
   video playback with Range support, one-shot reveal, rail, capture. Studies
   never touch it. A study is a TSL node material plus a config (palette,
   source clip, delays, surface). Playback is a dependency, never a problem.
2. **Three agents, three jobs.**
   - Builder (local model, cheap): writes one material and one config per
     iteration, renders three frames on the archive URL, and stops.
   - Critic (a vision model, local Qwen-VL or Fable): scores each iteration
     against the floor and the gates from the frames alone, blind to the
     builder's claims. Nothing cuts without a critic score. The builder never
     reads its own frame.
   - Curator (weekly, or the director): picks sources, sets the style of the
     week, writes the provocation.
3. **The iteration is the unit.** Ten minutes, one variable, three frames, a
   score. Best-of-N per hour goes live. The gallery is a grid of the last
   fifty iterations with their frames and scores, so progress is visible and
   a viewer can watch the search, not just the winner.
4. **Material as a palette.** Ten clips at a time from the ingest pipeline
   (Prelinger, NASA, live radar, webcams, Joel's own footage), each with a
   provenance row. The curator rotates them; the builder cannot pick the same
   clip twice in a row.
5. **A style deck.** Named looks as TSL presets with a source each: Rhodes
   (channel time), McCall (solid light), Turrell (Ganzfeld), Belson (form
   from luma), Paik (raster distortion), Conner (found-footage cut), Brakhage
   (painted frame). The builder composes from the deck; the curator adds one
   preset a week. Styles compound instead of restarting.
6. **Direction is one channel.** Joel and the director write into one
   Brain page the agents read at wake; nothing else counts as an order.
7. **The critic model is also the sweep.** The director's thirty-minute
   screenshot loop becomes the critic's job, every ten minutes, in code.
   The director returns to a daily pass that reads scores and frames, pushes
   one boundary, and gardens the deck.

## Canary

Three days, one clip, three presets, the locked rig. Success is fifty scored
iterations on the grid, at least ten passing the floor by the critic and
confirmed by the director's own frames, and zero cuts made on a claim.

## Name

Working title: Splice. A different repo, scaffolded from this one's rig code,
with a VISION written for the tournament shape.
